"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[8597],{78597:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"schemaVersion":1,"locale":"en","slug":"05-valyuta","title":"Lesson 05. The Ruble and Foreign Currency: The True Nature of Devaluation Fears","sourceFingerprint":"34333f5846d8b437580cdd5592f625708270ec098fe4b0a2a7621b52f64c7d8b","blocks":[{"id":"b001","markdown":"# Chapter 5. National Currency and the Foreign Exchange Shield: The True Nature of Devaluation Fears"},{"id":"b002","markdown":"> \\"Buying foreign currency at the height of market panic is the most expensive way to try to soothe frayed nerves.\\""},{"id":"b003","markdown":"The genetic memory of the domestic investor is literally steeped in tales of currency storms. The older generation remembers the default of 1998, when ruble savings vanished in practically a single quarter. Some retain vivid memories of December 2014 and the endless lines at currency exchange rate boards. And in the spring of 2022, millions of people frantically bought dollars at 120 ₽, only to discover quotes around 51–55 ₽ with bewilderment three months later."},{"id":"b004","markdown":"Such shocks gave rise to a persistent popular misconception: *\\"only hard foreign currency can protect capital\\"*. However, the dry figures of long-term returns over the past two decades paint a different picture: passively holding foreign banknotes consistently loses to domestic inflation. Foreign currency is not an all-purpose shield, but a specific tool that often merely masks risks."},{"id":"b005","markdown":"## Chronicle of Currency Storms: The Pendulum Always Swings Both Ways"},{"id":"b006","markdown":"The weakening of the national currency in Russia occurs in steps — swiftly and abruptly. However, a fundamental property of the currency market is that the chart moves in both directions."},{"id":"b007","markdown":"| Time Period | Exchange Rate Dynamics |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **Summer 1998 — early 1999** | Surge from around 6 ₽ to levels around 21 ₽ per US dollar |\\n| **January — December 2014** | Starting the year at 33 ₽, closing near 56 ₽ (at the peak of the December panic, the exchange tested 80 ₽) |\\n| **February — June 2022** | Jump from 75 ₽ to a record 120+ ₽ in March, followed by a deep plunge to 51 ₽ by early summer |\\n| **August — October 2023** | Regular breakouts above the psychological mark of 100 ₽ per dollar |\\n| **Late 2026 Benchmarks** | Dollar in the range of 81.80–82.00 ₽ (reference: 81.97 ₽), yuan — around 12.28 ₽ |"},{"id":"b008","markdown":"==The cardinal rule of a crisis market: emotionally buying currency at the peak of panic almost guaranteed locks in a local loss for months or years to come.=="},{"id":"b009","markdown":"## What Enemy Are We Building Defenses Against?"},{"id":"b010","markdown":"If a person\'s baseline income and daily life are centered inside the country, a direct drop in exchange rates does not hit them automatically. The blow is dealt indirectly:\\n1. Foreign medicines, electronics, cars, and trips abroad become more expensive.\\n2. Subsequently, imported components are factored into domestic production.\\n3. This entire chain transforms into overall **ruble inflation**."},{"id":"b011","markdown":"Thus, the true threat to capital is not the exchange rate itself, but the loss of purchasing power (the inflation tax). And against steady price increases, assets that generate real cash flow (productive businesses and bond coupons) work much more effectively than a stack of foreign banknotes locked in a safe."},{"id":"b012","markdown":"### When a Foreign Currency Position Is Vital:\\n* **Upcoming direct expenses abroad:** payment for educational programs, scheduled treatment in foreign clinics, purchase of rare imported equipment. A liability in yuan is safest to reserve in Chinese currency in advance — then market storms cease to matter.\\n* **Psychological buffer:** if lacking foreign currency causes sleepless nights, it is sensible to allocate a moderate share to it — **from 10% to 20% of the total portfolio**. It is important to realize: this is a fee for personal peace of mind and reduced anxiety, not a source of wealth generation."},{"id":"b013","markdown":"## Toolkit for Protection Against a Weakening Ruble"},{"id":"b014","markdown":"Savings can be protected in two ways: by holding the currency itself or by holding assets whose fundamental value naturally rises during a devaluation."},{"id":"b015","markdown":"| Instrument | Potential Return | Liquidity Level | Main Risk Factor |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Cash banknotes** | 0% (complete absence of cash flow) | Instant availability | Loss to issuing country\'s inflation, theft risk, predatory cash desk spreads |\\n| **CNY bank deposits** | Modest interest (1–3% per annum) | 1–2 business days (early withdrawal burns interest) | Bank limits, regulatory barriers, falling rates |\\n| **Foreign currency-denominated bonds** | Regular coupon income in hard value | Any trading day | Issuer credit risk, local liquidity deficit |\\n| **Gold (funds, unallocated metal accounts, exchange-traded metal)** | Capital appreciation | Any trading day | Volatility of global spot prices |\\n| **Securities of exporting companies** | Dividend stream + business scaling | Any trading day | Sanctions restrictions, fiscal pressure, commodity cycles |"},{"id":"b016","markdown":"### Implementation Details:\\n* **Foreign currency bank accounts:** Traditional dollar and euro accounts generate no yield and are subject to maintenance fees. As a reminder, funds deposited before March 9, 2022, are subject to a cash withdrawal limit of up to the equivalent of $10,000 (the restriction established by the Bank of Russia (CBR) has been extended until March 9, 2027).\\n* **Foreign currency-denominated bonds (including yuan issues):** Corporate borrowers and the Ministry of Finance (which debuted with yuan OFZ in December 2025) provide currency-linked payouts. Settlements and coupons are received in foreign currency or in rubles at the regulator\'s exchange rate, eliminating infrastructure bottlenecks.\\n* **Gold:** The domestic price of the precious metal is formed by multiplying the global dollar price by the current USD/RUB exchange rate. During a devaluation, the ruble price per gram rises automatically. Exchange-traded gold funds are vastly more efficient than physical bullion due to minimal spreads.\\n* **Export sector:** Oil and gas giants and metal producers generate foreign currency revenue while keeping operating expenses in rubles. Including a broad stock index already incorporates this protection; however, during times of geopolitical turmoil, a synchronized market decline can temporarily break this correlation.\\n* **Digital assets:** Stablecoins (USDT) offer mobility and a dollar peg, but carry technological and wallet regulatory risks."},{"id":"b017","markdown":"## \uD83E\uDDEE Back-of-the-Napkin Calculation"},{"id":"b018","markdown":"Let\'s examine how the protective buffer works using the example of a capital of **1,000,000 ₽**, where the investor pre-allocated **15% (150,000 ₽)** to currency-linked instruments."},{"id":"b019","markdown":"Let\'s imagine a scenario: over six months, the dollar rises on the FX market by **30%**:\\n1. The foreign currency portion of the portfolio (150,000 ₽) increases by 30%:\\n   $$150\\\\,000 \\\\times 1{,}30 = 195\\\\,000\\\\text{ ₽}\\\\quad (\\\\text{net gain } +45\\\\,000\\\\text{ ₽})$$\\n2. The ruble portion (850,000 ₽) is placed in fixed-income instruments and does not change in nominal value (excluding planned interest).\\n3. Total portfolio volume amounted to:\\n   $$850\\\\,000 + 195\\\\,000 = 1\\\\,045\\\\,000\\\\text{ ₽}$$\\n4. Total currency gain on capital:\\n   $$\\\\frac{45\\\\,000}{1\\\\,000\\\\,000} \\\\times 100\\\\% = +4{,}5\\\\%$$"},{"id":"b020","markdown":"**Bottom Line:** The defensive block automatically generated **+45,000 ₽ (+4.5% to the total portfolio)**, partially offsetting the price surge on imported electronics and medications, which was the very reason this share was factored into the system."}],"terms":[{"id":"valyutnyj-shit","term":"Foreign Currency Shield","definition":"A portion of a portfolio hedging against devaluation through gold and foreign currency assets."},{"id":"zameshchayushchie-obligatsii","term":"Replacement Bonds","definition":"Bonds linked to a foreign currency exchange rate, but settled in rubles."},{"id":"tokinizirovannoe-zoloto","term":"Tokenized Gold (#PAXG)","definition":"A digital on-chain asset backed by 1 troy ounce of physical gold."}],"quizzes":[{"id":"q1","q":"You permanently reside in Russia, spend exclusively rubles, and do not plan any trips abroad. What poses the greatest risk to your capital over a 10-year horizon?","options":["The drop in the ruble-to-dollar exchange rate on the market in and of itself.","Overall inflation, which absorbs the consequences of devaluation and erodes purchasing power.","There are no risks if cash is kept in a safe deposit box."],"correct":1,"explanation":"B. The exchange rate is merely one of the factors exerting pressure on prices. Inflation depreciates idle money regardless of the currency printed on the banknotes."},{"id":"q2","q":"In which situation does acquiring foreign cash carry the greatest risk for an investor\'s balance sheet?","options":["Purchasing according to a pre-approved plan in equal installments as part of a quarterly rebalancing.","Acquiring currency at the peak of media hype and sharp price spikes at exchange booths.","Buying yuan to cover a tuition payment scheduled in 6 months."],"correct":1,"explanation":"B. Emotional transactions during panic are made at extreme prices with maximum exchange booth spreads, which inevitably leads to a prolonged drawdown."},{"id":"q3","q":"How does capital protection via exchange-traded gold or replacement bonds work?","options":["They guarantee a fixed ruble return of 50% per annum under any circumstances.","Their value is legally fixed by the Bank of Russia and does not change.","Their valuation is automatically recalculated to reflect global prices or foreign exchange rates, protecting ruble purchasing power without the risk of holding physical cash."],"correct":1,"explanation":"C. These assets translate the dynamics of the global market and foreign exchange rates directly into the ruble value of the asset in a brokerage account."}],"officialSources":[]}')}}]);